@paulidale I have another change ready, but it's perplexing where to go. The NonStop Itanium platform has a similar randomize approach to `rand_unix.c`, although that method has everything folded together and it's suitable. The approach is a real time clock in nanoseconds and a system clock in microseconds. There is a suitable method to combine those without sleeping. I don't really think putting the code into `rand_unix.c` makes a lot of sense without a complete rewrite. And putting it in `rand_cpu_x86.c`, which easy, is somewhat misleading. Do you have a suggestion on this? Essentially, the code is:
```
unsigned long get_sys_random(void) {
        unsigned long long nanos = TS_NANOSECS_() % UINT_MAX ;
        unsigned long long micros = JULIANTIMESTAMP() % UINT_MAX;
        unsigned long long swapped_micros = (micros % 65536LL << 16LL)
                + (micros / 65536LL);
        unsigned long long random = (nanos * swapped_micros) % UINT_MAX;
        return (unsigned long) random;
}
```

There are two places for the timer to go: `rand_tsc.c` is the randomness source based on timers.  We currently do not allow this to be used because we can't quantify the amount of randomness produced.

It can also go in `get_timer_bits()` in `rand_unix.c` -- there is already special case code for Sun and HP/UX where a high resolution timer call is available.  This is called by `rand_pool_add_additional_data()` which mixes things up but doesn't count towards the randomness available.

If the timer is all you've got, you're out of luck for a seed source.  Better make sure that kernel random number generator is working.


As for the function that collects values:
``` C
unsigned long get_sys_random(void) {
        unsigned long long nanos = TS_NANOSECS_() % UINT_MAX ;
        unsigned long long micros = JULIANTIMESTAMP() % UINT_MAX;
        unsigned long long swapped_micros = (micros % 65536LL << 16LL)
                + (micros / 65536LL);
        unsigned long long random = (nanos * swapped_micros) % UINT_MAX;
        return (unsigned long) random;
}
```

My opinion is that this is attempting to obfuscate things and that it isn't producing anything even remotely close to cryptographic level randomness.  The variables nanos and micros will be highly correlated (since they are measuring the same thing).  The word swapping does nothing for the randomness.  Likewise the multiplication.  I'd just use `TS_NANOSECS_()`:
``` C
uint64_t get_timer_bits(void) {
    return TS_NANOSECS_();
}
```

Any entropy that is present will be in the low order bits of the TS_NANOSECS_().  So maybe `TS_NANOSECS_() & 15`.  The exact number of bits is difficult to estimate: it requires data collection and a lengthy statistical analysis and even then it could be wrong.

